\vspace{-5pt}
\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
\looseness=-1 CIVO propagates through multiple pathways: Walker Walk develops a $1.63\times$ reward prediction gap while Cheetah Run shows none ($1.00\times$) despite higher severity ($8.27\times$ NVE). The mechanism is analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced; unlike MPC, imagination-based actor-critic bootstraps value targets entirely within the learned model without real-data correction (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}). Frozen embeddings participate identically in the forward pass yet never trigger CIVO (0/16 controls vs.\ 5/7 trained, $p < 0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:causal_ablation}): the vulnerability lies in what the embedding \emph{learns}, not in conditioning architecture.

\vspace{-2pt}
\paragraph{Why interventions fail; practical implications.}
\label{sec:detector}
\looseness=-1 CIVO is an emergent property of co-adapting embeddings, not a single-pathway defect: SGS fails from pathway redundancy; RAVE fails as the EMA anchor tracks biased values; TCN shows binary behavior suggesting a phase transition. Wide dual-critic achieves prevention in 3/4 seeds (NVE $\leq 1.4\times$), though cross-environment transfer is limited. The structural vulnerability is the closed-loop nature of imagination: the same model parameters generate both the critic's training distribution and its bootstrap targets, so bias in $\dt{>}1$ predictions compounds without external correction---a self-reinforcing cycle that single-pathway interventions cannot break. These findings generalize beyond DreamerV3: embedding co-adaptation may interact pathologically with value estimation in any imagination-based actor-critic that bootstraps within a learned model.

\vspace{-2pt}
\paragraph{Environment complexity boundary.}
\looseness=-1 Cross-environment analysis across six DMC tasks spanning 1--21 DoF reveals that CIVO occupies a complexity Goldilocks zone: 1-DoF (Cartpole, 0/3) and 4-DoF (Hopper, 0/1) never trigger onset, while 12-DoF (Quadruped) and 21-DoF (Humanoid) baselines exhibit intrinsic value overestimation (NVE $4.7\times$ and $12.97\times$ without the gate mechanism) that precludes CIVO isolation. Onset occurs exclusively at 6 DoF (Walker 60\%, Cheetah 75\%; Table~\ref{tab:cross_env_civo}), suggesting CIVO requires sufficient dynamic complexity for temporal embeddings to develop exploitable value bias, yet a stable baseline where gate-induced perturbation dominates. For transformer-based world models \citep{micheli2023iris}, whether this boundary shifts remains open.

